course: everyday
lesson: 396
title: Mia gets the newest console
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On her birthday Mia tore off the wrapping paper, saw the newest console and screamed so loudly that her dog shot under the sofa and would not come out until teatime. By Monday half of Year 5 wanted to come round, and her mum had to stick a list on the fridge. Leo, who had never said a word to Mia, suddenly saved her a seat at lunch every single day. But he only ever asked about the console, and after a while Mia felt a bit hollow.
explain: Mia's mum said four friends at a time. On Saturday nine turned up at the door. Five went home grumpy, Mia went red, and her mum made a rota so everybody got a Saturday. Meanwhile Ruby, Mia's best friend since Reception, was squashed onto the arm of the sofa by six new kids. She went quiet. Mia noticed and kept one Saturday morning just for the two of them, with pancakes and no crowd.
explain: The first week had its disasters. Jayden knocked orange squash over the brand new controller. Mia felt a flash of real anger, and Jayden felt terrible and said sorry at once. Mia's dad helped them dry it with a hairdryer on the cool setting, it worked again, and Jayden bought her a controller skin with his pocket money. Then the fan got loud and Leo yelled «it's going to explode!» Mia panicked and pulled the plug in the middle of a save. The save was gone. Her dad showed her the console just needed space around it for air, and they moved it off the carpet.
explain: Not everyone was happy for her. Ollie said he had the newest console too, but his was the old one with a sticker on it, and he felt ashamed and scared that someone would come round and see. When Kai went round and said «oh cool, that one has the good racing game», nobody cared at all. Theo said Mia only got it because her parents were rich. Mia wanted to say something mean back, but she calmly told him the truth: they had saved for months and skipped a holiday. Her little cousin called her «console girl», and it caught on, so Mia announced she also wanted to be known for cartwheels and did one in the playground to prove it.
explain: The best moment of the month? Grandma asked for a go, held the controller upside down, won one race completely by accident and talked about it at every family dinner for a month.
fact: A new thing feels best when the people you like get to enjoy it too.
fact: Some people want to be your friend because of something you own; it is fair to notice who likes you and who likes your stuff.
fact: Old friends can feel pushed out when a house suddenly becomes popular.
fact: A host can say «not today» kindly and offer another day instead.
fact: Owning an accident and saying sorry quickly is what friends remember.
fact: Consoles need space around them so air can cool them; a loud fan usually means it is warm, not that it will explode.
fact: Switching a console off at the plug in the middle of a save can lose the game you were playing.
fact: Lying about what you own to keep up usually makes you more nervous than the thing ever would.
fact: Jealousy often comes out as a mean guess, and a calm, true answer works better than a mean one back.
fact: A thing you own is not the whole of who you are.
exercise: How did Mia feel when she unwrapped the console? | Like the luckiest girl in the world | Bored, because she really wanted a bike | Scared, because it was so very expensive | Cross, because it was the wrong colour
exercise: Why did Mia's dog hide under the sofa? | Mia screamed with joy very loudly | The new console made a loud beeping noise | Grandma dropped the heavy box on it | The rustly wrapping paper scared it
exercise: Leo had never spoken to Mia, then saved her a seat every day. How did Mia feel later? | A bit hollow, as he only asked about the console | Proud, because he was her best friend now | Angry, because she wanted to sit alone | Happy, because he loved her drawings
exercise: Why did Ruby go quiet? | She felt pushed out by the new crowd | She did not like any kind of video game at all | She had fallen out with Mia's mum | She wanted a console of her own
exercise: What did Mia do for Ruby? | Kept one Saturday morning just for the two of them | Told all the new kids to stop coming round to her house | Gave Ruby the console to take home | Let Ruby be in charge of the rota
exercise: Nine friends came when only four were allowed. What helped in the end? | A rota so everyone got a Saturday | Letting all nine kids in every single week | Choosing only the very best players | Telling the five extra kids off for coming
exercise: What did Jayden do right after spilling squash on the controller? | Said sorry fast and offered to help | Ran home before anyone noticed | Blamed Mia's dog for the spill | Said it was already broken
exercise: Why was it a mistake to pull the plug when the fan got loud? | The save was lost, and the console only needed air | The console would have exploded anyway, fan or no fan | Pulling the plug always breaks consoles | It woke up Mia's dog under the sofa
exercise: Where should a console sit so it does not get too hot? | Somewhere with space around it for air | On a thick soft carpet to keep it from getting scratched | Inside a cupboard with the door shut tight | Under a blanket to keep all the dust off
exercise: How did Ollie feel about his old console with a sticker? | Ashamed, and scared someone would come and see | Proud, because old consoles are worth much more money | Bored, because he never plays it | Happy, because the sticker looked real
exercise: What happened when Kai saw Ollie's old console? | He liked it, and nobody cared that it was old | He told everyone at school that Ollie was a liar | He laughed so hard that Ollie cried | He refused to ever go round again
exercise: Theo said Mia's parents were rich. What did Mia do? | Told him calmly they had saved for months | Said his parents were poor and mean, so it was his fault | Said sorry for having the console | Stopped speaking to him for a year
exercise: Why did Mia do a cartwheel in the playground? | To be known for more than her console | To show off in front of Leo and his friends | Because Theo dared her to | Because she had won a race
exercise: What did Grandma do after winning a race by accident? | Talked about it at every family dinner for a month | Said she never wanted to play it again | Bought a console of her own the very next day after school | Gave the controller back upside down
exercise: Which is the best way to share excitement about a new present? | Enjoy it and share it without showing off | Keep it secret so nobody ever gets jealous of you | Talk about how much it cost | Only let the coolest kids have a go
exercise: Why might the name «console girl» annoy Mia? | She is much more than the thing she owns | She does not really like the console any more | It is a rude word in her family | Her cousin is not allowed to give people names
test: «mate, ur console's screaming, it's gonna blow up». Mia's dad would say? | It needs air round it, move it off the carpet | Yeah, unplug it right now and run out of the room | Pour some water on it to cool it | Ignore it, all consoles do that forever
test: «i got the new one too» — it's the old one with a sticker. What's the lesson's point? | Lying to keep up makes you more nervous | The sticker makes it the new console | Everyone lies about consoles, so it's fine | Only new consoles have good games
test: Theo: «only got it cos ur mum n dad are loaded». Best comeback? | The calm truth: they saved for months | «At least we're not poor like you, so shut up» | «Yeah, we're rich, so what» | Push him and walk off
test: Nine kids at the door, mum says four. «just let us all in, go on». Kindest answer? | «Not today, but you're on the rota next week» | «Only my best four, the rest go away» | «Fine, mum won't notice» | «Whoever pushes in first gets in»
test: «oops lol squash all over ur controller» — Jayden laughs and walks off. What should he have done? | Said sorry and helped put it right | Laughed louder so it all seemed funny | Hidden the controller under the sofa | Blamed the dog for knocking it over
test: «why's Leo suddenly ur best mate?? he never talked to u before». What's fair to think? | He might like the console more than me, so I'll notice | He must have always secretly liked me and was too shy | He's definitely a bad person | I should give him the console
test: Ruby's sitting on the sofa arm, not talking. «u ok?» What does she most likely feel? | Pushed out by all the new kids | Too hot from the console fan all day | Tired of playing all day | Cross with Mia's grandma
test: Gran: «which way up does this thing go, love?» What's the wise move? | Show her patiently, everyone starts upside down | Laugh and take it off her | Tell her it's only for kids, not grans | Give her the unplugged controller
test: «fan's loud, just yank the plug». What happened when Mia did that? | She lost the game she had not saved | The console blew up anyway, even with air | Nothing, it was all saved | The fan got even louder
test: Teacher: «Mia, are you console girl now?» Mia: «I do cartwheels too.» Why say that? | You can choose what you want to be known for | Teachers don't like consoles and told her to stop | She wanted to stop playing games | Cartwheels are cooler than consoles
test: «my sofa fits 5 and 6 more kids want to come every Saturday». Which plan is fair? | Take turns with a rota | Let in whoever brings sweets | Only invite the best at games | Stop inviting anyone at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the console of the pupil Mia emitted a loud noise from its fan. | It was warm and required space around it for ventilation | It was about to explode imminently | It had been damaged by orange squash spilled upon it earlier | It was operating in a defective manner from purchase
exam: It is given that a classmate asserts that a pupil obtained a costly console solely by reason of parental wealth. Select the response most consistent with the guidance of this lesson. | A calm statement of the true circumstances | A retaliatory remark concerning the classmate's family | An apology for possessing the console | A refusal to address the classmate thereafter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct appropriate subsequent to accidentally damaging the property of a friend? | Prompt acknowledgement, apology and an offer to make amends | Immediate departure from the premises | Attribution of responsibility to a domestic animal present at the time | Denial that any damage has been occasioned
exam: Whereas a pupil's residence has become popular among numerous new visitors, state the course of action most likely to preserve a long-standing friendship. | The reservation of time exclusively for the long-standing friend | The exclusion of all new visitors permanently | The cessation of all social gatherings | The provision of the console to the friend
